==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|in-|numerable}}; from {{inh|en|enm|innumerable}}, from {{der|en|la|innumerābilis}}, from {{af|la|in-|numerābilis|nocat=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-us-innumerable.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnuːməɹ.əbəl/}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈnjuːməɹ.əbəl/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not capable of being [[counted]], [[enumerated]], or [[numbered]], hence, indefinitely numerous; of great number.
#: {{ux|en|The casualties of the Second World War were so great that they are '''innumerable'''.}}
#* '''1889''', {{w|Mark Twain}}, ''{{w|A Connecticut Yankee in King Arthur's Court}}''
#*: Soon we could see the '''innumerable''' banners fluttering, and then the sun struck the sea of armor and set it all aflash.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|countless}}, {{l|en|numberless}}, {{l|en|unnumbered}}, {{l|en|untold}}; see also [[Thesaurus:innumerable]]

====Translations====
{{trans-see|not capable of being counted|countless}}

